// Fixture: indentation_nested_scope_differs_from_parent_error
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §3 -- RESOLVED (previously flagged, now written into
// the spec): a "scope" for the single-indentation-character rule means one ENTIRE
// top-level `define`/instance block, including everything nested inside it -- not each
// individual nested field block independently. A nested block cannot switch
// indentation characters from its parent, even if that nested block is internally
// self-consistent on its own.
//
// This fixture was originally built the other way (as a POSITIVE fixture, expecting
// sibling nested blocks to legally differ from each other and from their parent) --
// that reading was wrong, per the resolution above. Rebuilt as the negative case the
// resolution actually implies: a genuinely different failure shape from
// indentation_mixed_tabs_spaces_error.gddl, which mixes tab/space between two SIBLING
// statements at the SAME nesting depth. THIS fixture mixes across nesting DEPTHS --
// the top-level `branch` line is tab-indented; its own nested body (`x`, `y`) is
// space-indented. That nested body is internally consistent (both its lines use
// spaces), which is exactly the case that needed clarifying: internal self-consistency
// of a nested block does NOT exempt it from matching its parent scope's character.

define Level2
	x = u32
	y = u32

define Level1
	branch = Level2

Level1 Holder
	branch
        x = 1
        y = 2
// ERROR: `branch` (above) is tab-indented, at Holder's top-level scope. `x` and `y`
// (above) are space-indented -- internally consistent with EACH OTHER, but not with
// `branch`'s tab, and both belong to the same overarching scope (Holder's entire
// instance body, nested content included) per the resolved rule. This must be a
// compile error even though the nested block, taken alone, looks perfectly consistent.

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compile error: mixed indentation characters within a single scope (Holder's whole
//   instance body counts as one scope), reported at phase 3 (Parse), same category as
//   indentation_mixed_tabs_spaces_error.gddl -- but reached via a depth-mismatch
//   between a parent line and its nested block, not a same-depth sibling mismatch.
